  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for heavy rain and flooding Sunday-\nTuesday.\n\nStrengthening south-southwest flow in advance of a slow moving\nupper trough in the Ohio Valley will drive anomalous 1.5-2\" PW \ninto CPA along and ahead of an approaching cold front on Sunday.\nStrong warm advection and moisture transport/LLJ combined with \nfavorable upper dynamics should support active/elevated \nconvection reaching west-central PA as early as late Saturday \nnight with max POPs centered on the 24hr Sunday window. There \nremains model spread on the degree of instability built up by \ntomorrow evening, which will likely depend on how much sunshine\nis able to break through the clouds behind the morning \nprecipitation. So uncertainty remains regarding where the best \ncores of convection will end up and the coverage they will \noccupy. Should there be cloud breaks that raise CAPE values, \nadequate shear and lapse rates may allow for isolated damaging \nwind gusts in Western/Central PA. \n\nAs the upper level trough closes off/slows its forward progress\nover the Ohio River Valley, synoptic ascent should remain in \nplace. This will allow for daily heavy rain and possible \nflooding conditions to start next week. While WPC trimmed the \nlevel 2/4 excessive rain outlook back a bit over NW PA for \nSunday/D2, we will need to watch for sneaky +RA signal to shift \nover eastern portions of the CWA where the level 1 marginal risk\nwill now extends all the way to the East Coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Outlook for first half of August is warm and wet\n\nMedium and long range numerical weather prediction guidance\nsignals a prolonged stretch of rainy and warm (but not hot)\nweather across Pennsylvania for the first two weeks of August.\nEnsemble accumulated rainfall plots show a steady increase in\nrainfall amounts and a notable absence of prolonged dry spells.\nThis will be a significant difference from August of 2025, which\nsaw hot and extremely dry conditions to start August. In fact,\nmany locations saw zero rainfall in the first two weeks of\nAugust last year.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nJuly = record-setting month in terms of convective warnings. \nWFO CTP issued 12 Tornado Warnings in July, which is the most \nsince 1996 when we issued 25. 95 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is\nthe 3rd most in any July on record, behind 1996 (96) and 2021 \n(122). Year-to- date, 226 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is the \nmost on record, passing 2019 (221), and 24 Tornado Warnings is \n4th most behind 1998 (55), 2011 (37), and 1996 (30). 250 total \nSevere Thunderstorm Warnings + Tornado Warnings is the most on \nrecord year-to-date, passing 2019 (241).",
